WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.077
Welcome to Module 10, the last
module of the Data Platform and SQL

2
00:00:04.077 --> 00:00:07.912
Server Identity learning path:
deploying to test and production.

3
00:00:07.912 --> 00:00:11.762
It teaches Runbook R oh five, login
migration and the parallel run,

4
00:00:11.762 --> 00:00:15.662
and the break-glass design in
Appendix B of the data platform book,

5
00:00:15.662 --> 00:00:20.212
with Chapter 10 of the developer
workbook, Building SQL Server Identity.

6
00:00:20.212 --> 00:00:22.574
Everything in the earlier
modules ends here.

7
00:00:22.574 --> 00:00:25.090
Take this module after Module 9.

8
00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:30.227
Why does this matter?

9
00:00:30.227 --> 00:00:34.258
This is the change itself: one
instance's logins move from Windows

10
00:00:34.258 --> 00:00:38.339
to Microsoft Entra ID, first on a
test instance, then in production.

11
00:00:38.339 --> 00:00:42.377
The commands are the same both times;
only the settings file changes.

12
00:00:42.377 --> 00:00:44.241
Three rules hold it together.

13
00:00:44.241 --> 00:00:47.678
Nothing runs that a second
person didn't approve by hash.

14
00:00:47.678 --> 00:00:50.264
The way back is planned
before the cutover.

15
00:00:50.264 --> 00:00:53.612
And production's preflight
refuses to start until a real test

16
00:00:53.612 --> 00:00:56.827
stage for the same instance is
sealed in the evidence chain.

17
00:00:56.827 --> 00:01:00.700
In this module you rehearse that
whole test stage as a dry run.

18
00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:07.204
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

19
00:01:07.204 --> 00:01:09.600
which takes about seventy
minutes with the lab.

20
00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:11.994
You'll name what preflight checks,

21
00:01:11.994 --> 00:01:14.962
P F oh one to P F oh seven,
and what it can't see.

22
00:01:14.962 --> 00:01:18.404
You'll explain how three S
H A two fifty-six hashes tie

23
00:01:18.404 --> 00:01:22.087
the files a second person
approved to the files that run.

24
00:01:22.087 --> 00:01:25.252
You'll walk the test stage
from preflight to the seal,

25
00:01:25.252 --> 00:01:27.238
and say what makes it a go or a no-go.

26
00:01:27.238 --> 00:01:31.120
You'll explain why a dry-run
stage seals only as a rehearsal,

27
00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:34.437
and why production preflight
then fails P F oh six.

28
00:01:34.437 --> 00:01:38.425
And you'll name the rollback triggers,
and the path when Entra can't be reached.

29
00:01:38.425 --> 00:01:42.509
Before you start, finish Module 9:
its closure record and drift schedule

30
00:01:42.509 --> 00:01:46.800
run during the observation, and
production's seal needs the closure record.

31
00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:51.810
This stage also runs Module 4's
login plan and parallel run for real.

32
00:01:55.610 --> 00:01:58.385
Unit 2 starts with the
settings and preflight.

33
00:01:58.385 --> 00:02:00.333
One settings file per environment,

34
00:02:00.333 --> 00:02:04.604
filled from the template and kept inside
your boundary, names the instance,

35
00:02:04.604 --> 00:02:08.048
its snapshots, the exception
registry, the reviewed mapping,

36
00:02:08.048 --> 00:02:10.597
the evidence chain and
the break-glass group.

37
00:02:10.597 --> 00:02:12.148
No secret goes in it.

38
00:02:12.148 --> 00:02:14.185
Preflight then checks seven things.

39
00:02:14.185 --> 00:02:16.873
P F oh one, the settings are complete.

40
00:02:16.873 --> 00:02:18.977
Oh two, the cloud is Azure Cloud,

41
00:02:18.977 --> 00:02:23.773
the commercial cloud where a G C C
Moderate tenant's Arc resources are.

42
00:02:23.773 --> 00:02:27.372
Oh three, the instance is in
Arc-eligibility Category 1.

43
00:02:27.372 --> 00:02:30.273
Oh four, the mapping resolves every login.

44
00:02:30.273 --> 00:02:34.023
Oh five, the break-glass path
is in the exception registry.

45
00:02:34.023 --> 00:02:35.688
Oh six, for production only,

46
00:02:35.688 --> 00:02:39.623
a sealed test stage for the same
instance that isn't a rehearsal.

47
00:02:39.623 --> 00:02:42.758
And oh seven, the S A
account disabled and renamed,

48
00:02:42.758 --> 00:02:45.659
which is a warning and
doesn't stop the change.

49
00:02:45.659 --> 00:02:47.975
Preflight can't see the deployer's rights,

50
00:02:47.975 --> 00:02:51.272
so the deployer reads them
after activating the role.

51
00:02:55.070 --> 00:02:58.419
The plan writes three
files: create dot sequel,

52
00:02:58.419 --> 00:03:03.795
cutover dot sequel and rollback dot
sequel, each with its S H A two fifty-six.

53
00:03:03.795 --> 00:03:06.232
A second person reads
them line by line and

54
00:03:06.232 --> 00:03:08.933
records the three hashes
in the change record.

55
00:03:08.933 --> 00:03:13.188
When the deploy runs, a file that doesn't
hash to its approved value is refused

56
00:03:13.188 --> 00:03:17.646
before anything connects, so the files
that run are the files that were approved.

57
00:03:17.646 --> 00:03:20.489
The deployer signs in as
its own Entra account,

58
00:03:20.489 --> 00:03:25.354
a member of the instance's Entra admin
group, activated under pim for the change

59
00:03:25.354 --> 00:03:30.070
window only, so that sys-admin, alter any
login and control server all read one.

60
00:03:30.070 --> 00:03:34.043
And the book's Chapter 12, What the
Engine-Authentication Decision Settles,

61
00:03:34.043 --> 00:03:36.910
makes the parallel run
required for production.

62
00:03:40.710 --> 00:03:43.010
Unit 3 is the change itself.

63
00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:46.997
The create adds the Entra logins
and users beside the Windows ones,

64
00:03:46.997 --> 00:03:50.960
and disables nothing, so both paths
stay open for the parallel run.

65
00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:54.235
The parallel run is judged
against its exit criteria.

66
00:03:54.235 --> 00:03:58.897
The rollback plan is dry-run before the
cutover, so its record exists first.

67
00:03:58.897 --> 00:04:02.479
The cutover disables the
superseded users and SQL logins

68
00:04:02.479 --> 00:04:05.498
and denies connect sequel
to the superseded groups.

69
00:04:05.498 --> 00:04:07.047
It never drops anything.

70
00:04:07.047 --> 00:04:09.448
Then thirty days of observation follow.

71
00:04:09.448 --> 00:04:12.225
The rollback triggers are
set before the change:

72
00:04:12.225 --> 00:04:16.462
any failed login on a just-disabled or
just-denied login from a production

73
00:04:16.462 --> 00:04:19.727
application, any job or linked
server failing to connect,

74
00:04:19.727 --> 00:04:23.547
or an application owner reporting
an outage traced to the cutover.

75
00:04:23.547 --> 00:04:25.336
Any one stops the stage.

76
00:04:25.336 --> 00:04:29.916
Runbook R oh five keeps every cutover
reversible: the Windows database users

77
00:04:29.916 --> 00:04:34.530
are still there, so the approved rollback
opens the old path again at once.

78
00:04:38.330 --> 00:04:41.192
The test stage is a go
when five things hold.

79
00:04:41.192 --> 00:04:45.455
Preflight passed, and the deployer's
rights read one, one, one.

80
00:04:45.455 --> 00:04:49.080
The three hashes in the change
record match the files that ran.

81
00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:52.055
The parallel run printed
cutover-ready, N of N.

82
00:04:52.055 --> 00:04:55.353
Thirty days of observation
passed with no failed

83
00:04:55.353 --> 00:04:58.730
login on a disabled or denied
login, and no drift row.

84
00:04:58.730 --> 00:05:02.180
And every deploy record
says applied, not dry run.

85
00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:05.905
A no-go is a finding with an
owner, and the stage is repeated.

86
00:05:05.905 --> 00:05:08.012
The seal re-hashes every run's files,

87
00:05:08.012 --> 00:05:12.734
refuses a stage missing a required step
or with a deploy run that wasn't applied,

88
00:05:12.734 --> 00:05:16.730
and appends one record to the chain
with the previous record's hash.

89
00:05:16.730 --> 00:05:19.993
Sealed with the rehearsal
switch, it records a rehearsal,

90
00:05:19.993 --> 00:05:22.493
which production preflight never accepts.

91
00:05:22.493 --> 00:05:24.575
Production then runs the same steps,

92
00:05:24.575 --> 00:05:27.768
and its seal also needs the
closure record from Module 9.

93
00:05:27.768 --> 00:05:32.000
When Entra can't be reached, Appendix
B's path is the registered break-glass

94
00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:36.393
Windows group, empty by default, with
every membership change alerting.

95
00:05:40.190 --> 00:05:41.077
Try it.

96
00:05:41.077 --> 00:05:45.572
Lab 1 rehearses the whole test stage on
the kit's example settings, as dry runs:

97
00:05:45.572 --> 00:05:49.828
nothing connects to an instance, and
nothing runs with the apply switch.

98
00:05:49.828 --> 00:05:54.690
Open PowerShell 7, go to the kit's folder,
and make this run's results folder.

99
00:05:54.690 --> 00:05:58.910
Step one copies the kit's two example
settings files, test and production,

100
00:05:58.910 --> 00:06:02.216
into the run folder, and points
their evidence chain at it,

101
00:06:02.216 --> 00:06:04.678
so the kit's folder
stays as you unzipped it.

102
00:06:04.678 --> 00:06:07.539
They describe the fixtures'
sequel P R D oh one.

103
00:06:07.539 --> 00:06:09.890
Step two preflights the test stage.

104
00:06:09.890 --> 00:06:12.635
Every check passes,
and P F oh seven warns:

105
00:06:12.635 --> 00:06:17.377
the example's S A account is still
enabled under its own name on day one.

106
00:06:17.377 --> 00:06:19.053
It doesn't stop the change.

107
00:06:19.053 --> 00:06:22.820
P F oh six isn't run, because
it's for production only.

108
00:06:26.620 --> 00:06:30.920
Step three runs the audit and the Arc
check on the snapshots the settings name.

109
00:06:30.920 --> 00:06:34.795
In a real stage these are the instance's
own snapshots, taken that day.

110
00:06:34.795 --> 00:06:39.808
The audit flags sequel P R D oh one's S
A account, enabled under its own name,

111
00:06:39.808 --> 00:06:43.631
and a usable SQL login, and
the Arc check puts sequel

112
00:06:43.631 --> 00:06:46.945
P R D oh one in Category 1:
ready to plan the logins.

113
00:06:46.945 --> 00:06:48.845
Step four plans the change.

114
00:06:48.845 --> 00:06:53.361
It writes three sequel files: the
create, with sixteen statements;

115
00:06:53.361 --> 00:06:56.707
the cutover, with five; and
the rollback, with five.

116
00:06:56.707 --> 00:06:59.445
Each has its S H A two fifty-six.

117
00:06:59.445 --> 00:07:02.920
Those are the three values
a second person approves.

118
00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:08.883
Step five approves the hashes.

119
00:07:08.883 --> 00:07:12.093
The approver re-hashes the
three files after reading them,

120
00:07:12.093 --> 00:07:16.170
and this lab keeps each hash in a
variable, as a change record would keep it.

121
00:07:16.170 --> 00:07:19.620
Step six dry-runs the create
phase with the approved hash.

122
00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:22.194
Without the apply switch
it connects to nothing:

123
00:07:22.194 --> 00:07:25.670
it checks the hash and prints the
sixteen statements it would run.

124
00:07:25.670 --> 00:07:29.043
Create login from external
provider for each Entra login,

125
00:07:29.043 --> 00:07:33.682
the Entra admin group added to sys-admin,
then the database users and their roles.

126
00:07:33.682 --> 00:07:38.169
Step seven tries a hash that isn't
the approved one, sixty-four zeros.

127
00:07:38.169 --> 00:07:42.750
The deploy stops before anything
connects: re-plan and re-approve.

128
00:07:46.550 --> 00:07:49.950
Step eight judges the parallel
run on day thirty's snapshot.

129
00:07:49.950 --> 00:07:53.086
In the fixtures, day thirty
is after the cutover,

130
00:07:53.086 --> 00:07:57.988
so each legacy login reads as already
cut over: cutover-ready, four of four.

131
00:07:57.988 --> 00:08:03.099
Step nine dry-runs the rollback, before
the cutover, so its record exists first.

132
00:08:03.099 --> 00:08:07.712
It would grant connect sequel back to the
groups, and enable the superseded logins.

133
00:08:07.712 --> 00:08:09.762
Step ten dry-runs the cutover.

134
00:08:09.762 --> 00:08:14.549
Users and SQL logins are disabled,
and groups are denied connect sequel.

135
00:08:14.549 --> 00:08:17.945
The Windows admin group leaves
the sys-admin role first,

136
00:08:17.945 --> 00:08:20.675
because a deny doesn't
bind sys-admin members.

137
00:08:20.675 --> 00:08:24.740
Nothing is dropped, which is why
the rollback can reverse it at once.

138
00:08:28.540 --> 00:08:31.978
Step eleven tries to seal the
stage without the parallel run.

139
00:08:31.978 --> 00:08:34.797
The seal refuses a stage
missing a step the kit's

140
00:08:34.797 --> 00:08:37.815
requirements file requires,
and writes no record.

141
00:08:37.815 --> 00:08:40.277
Step twelve tries to seal the whole stage.

142
00:08:40.277 --> 00:08:43.555
Every step is there, but the
deployments were dry runs,

143
00:08:43.555 --> 00:08:47.503
so the seal refuses again, and
points you to the rehearsal switch.

144
00:08:47.503 --> 00:08:50.527
Step thirteen runs the same
command with that switch.

145
00:08:50.527 --> 00:08:53.129
It writes the record,
marked as a rehearsal,

146
00:08:53.129 --> 00:08:57.589
with the eight steps it sealed and no
previous record, since it's the first.

147
00:08:57.589 --> 00:09:00.260
The record's short hash
differs from run to run,

148
00:09:00.260 --> 00:09:03.110
because the record names
this run's folders.

149
00:09:06.910 --> 00:09:09.247
Step fourteen preflights production.

150
00:09:09.247 --> 00:09:12.560
P F oh one to oh five
pass, as they did for test.

151
00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:15.595
P F oh six fails: the chain
holds only a rehearsal,

152
00:09:15.595 --> 00:09:19.097
and production needs a sealed
test stage that isn't one.

153
00:09:19.097 --> 00:09:22.184
Preflight says stop here,
and exits with code one.

154
00:09:22.184 --> 00:09:26.297
Step fifteen runs the deployment
tests: ten pass, none fail.

155
00:09:26.297 --> 00:09:28.286
Step sixteen lists your runs.

156
00:09:28.286 --> 00:09:32.060
There's nothing to clean up: this
run's results stay in the run folder.

157
00:09:32.060 --> 00:09:36.401
Check your work: you can walk the test
stage from preflight to the seal,

158
00:09:36.401 --> 00:09:41.351
and say why a wrong hash is refused before
anything connects, why a dry-run stage

159
00:09:41.351 --> 00:09:46.372
seals only as a rehearsal, and why
production preflight then fails P F oh six.

160
00:09:50.170 --> 00:09:52.820
This module has no Lab 2 of its own.

161
00:09:52.820 --> 00:09:55.737
The real test-instance run
is Chapter 10's Stage 1,

162
00:09:55.737 --> 00:09:59.373
and it wasn't run by the authors:
every command has run only as

163
00:09:59.373 --> 00:10:03.082
a dry run on the kit's example
settings and in its Pester tests.

164
00:10:03.082 --> 00:10:07.307
You need a test instance in Arc-eligibility
Category 1, never production;

165
00:10:07.307 --> 00:10:11.461
a deployer activated under pim for
the change window only; Reader on the

166
00:10:11.461 --> 00:10:15.545
subscriptions to read Arc; and the
tools signed in to commercial Azure.

167
00:10:15.545 --> 00:10:19.843
You also need a filled test settings
file with no secret, a reviewed mapping,

168
00:10:19.843 --> 00:10:22.754
the exception registry with
the break-glass group,

169
00:10:22.754 --> 00:10:25.457
and a second person to
approve the three hashes.

170
00:10:25.457 --> 00:10:28.236
The run is Lab 1's steps
against your test instance,

171
00:10:28.236 --> 00:10:32.030
with the apply switch on the create
and the cutover, the parallel run

172
00:10:32.030 --> 00:10:36.095
and thirty days of observation, then
a seal without the rehearsal switch.

173
00:10:36.095 --> 00:10:39.084
To back it out, run the
approved rollback with apply,

174
00:10:39.084 --> 00:10:42.145
under its own change record
and in its own run folder.

175
00:10:42.145 --> 00:10:47.200
Run the test stage first, read every
file you approve, and keep the evidence.

176
00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:52.600
The federal requirement.

177
00:10:52.600 --> 00:10:57.125
Chapter 10 maps each
step to NIST SP 800-53.

178
00:10:57.125 --> 00:11:02.325
Preflight supports A C six, least privilege,
and C M six, configuration settings:

179
00:11:02.325 --> 00:11:07.125
keep the preflight file and its manifest,
with the deployer's rights read.

180
00:11:07.125 --> 00:11:10.613
The plan and its approval
support C M three and C M four,

181
00:11:10.613 --> 00:11:14.893
configuration change control and
impact analysis: keep the plan,

182
00:11:14.893 --> 00:11:19.887
the three sequel files, and the hashes
in the change record with the approver.

183
00:11:19.887 --> 00:11:23.532
The create and the cutover
support C M three and A C two:

184
00:11:23.532 --> 00:11:27.838
keep each deploy record with mode
applied and the hash that ran.

185
00:11:27.838 --> 00:11:32.535
The rollback plan supports C M three
and C P two, the contingency plan:

186
00:11:32.535 --> 00:11:36.225
keep the rollback dry run,
written before the cutover.

187
00:11:36.225 --> 00:11:41.278
And the seal supports C A seven and A U
nine, protection of audit information:

188
00:11:41.278 --> 00:11:44.375
keep the chain, each record
holding the S H A two

189
00:11:44.375 --> 00:11:48.287
fifty-six of every manifest
and the previous record's hash.

190
00:11:48.287 --> 00:11:52.485
K S Is are FedRAMP twenty X indicators
for cloud service providers:

191
00:11:52.485 --> 00:11:56.300
your cloud providers report
against them, the agency doesn't.

192
00:11:56.300 --> 00:12:00.660
These records provide evidence for
the change; the assessor decides.

193
00:12:04.460 --> 00:12:06.510
Check yourself with two questions.

194
00:12:06.510 --> 00:12:09.109
First: you run the create
with an approved hash

195
00:12:09.109 --> 00:12:11.548
that isn't the one the
create file hashes to.

196
00:12:11.548 --> 00:12:12.586
What happens?

197
00:12:12.586 --> 00:12:16.447
The answer is b: it's refused
before anything connects.

198
00:12:16.447 --> 00:12:18.298
Re-plan and re-approve.

199
00:12:18.298 --> 00:12:21.586
Second: the chain holds
only the rehearsal record.

200
00:12:21.586 --> 00:12:23.723
What does production preflight report?

201
00:12:23.723 --> 00:12:27.436
The answer is b: P F oh six
fails, because there's no sealed

202
00:12:27.436 --> 00:12:31.561
test-stage record for sequel P R
D oh one that isn't a rehearsal.

203
00:12:31.561 --> 00:12:32.598
Stop here.

204
00:12:32.598 --> 00:12:37.250
The module page has two more questions,
each linked to the section that answers it.

205
00:12:41.050 --> 00:12:42.075
To sum up.

206
00:12:42.075 --> 00:12:45.774
You rehearsed one instance's login
migration through the test stage,

207
00:12:45.774 --> 00:12:49.852
from preflight and the approved hashes
to the parallel run, the cutover and

208
00:12:49.852 --> 00:12:54.062
its rollback, and sealed it as a rehearsal
that production preflight refuses.

209
00:12:54.062 --> 00:12:57.844
A real test stage, applied and
sealed without the rehearsal switch,

210
00:12:57.844 --> 00:13:02.588
is what lets production start, with the same
commands and a different settings file.

211
00:13:02.588 --> 00:13:05.587
Before shipping, work through
the Chapter 10 checklist.

212
00:13:05.587 --> 00:13:09.649
That's the end of the Data Platform
and SQL Server Identity learning path.

213
00:13:09.649 --> 00:13:13.090
Go back to the learning path's
index for the modules and the code.
